WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.236 --> 00:00:35.183
Hi, everyone.

00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:37.850
My name is Brandon Leshchinskiy,
and I'm a graduate student here

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:38.690
at MIT.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.400
Now the presentation
that you're watching

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.802
was created by an organization
here at MIT called Earth DNA,

00:00:43.802 --> 00:00:46.010
but it's going to be shared
with high school students

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.220
all around the country.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.595
And so I'm really excited to
be sharing it with you guys.

00:00:49.595 --> 00:00:50.162
Welcome.

00:00:50.162 --> 00:00:52.370
This talk is going to have
some audience interaction.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.495
So to get everybody warmed
up, please just yell out

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:55.680
your own name on three.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.180
Ready?

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.110
One, two, three.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.760
[ALL YELLING]

00:00:58.760 --> 00:00:59.720
Nice to meet you.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.757
Like I said, I'm Brandon.

00:01:02.757 --> 00:01:04.340
Now in this talk,
we're going to cover

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:09.080
four things: the truth, the
consequences, the myths,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.442
and the future.

00:01:10.442 --> 00:01:11.900
And I want to start
with the truth.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.125
In particular, let's review the
science of what's happening.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:16.250
We'll go through it slowly.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.507
In 1896, a scientist discovered
the greenhouse effect.

00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:23.840
Can somebody here tell me
what the greenhouse effect is?

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.160
Does anyone here know?

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:26.660
Yes?

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.910
AUDIENCE: It's the part of
the atmosphere sun's rays

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.857
go inside it and then
it's difficult for them

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:33.190
to get outside of it, so it
keeps the earth's atmosphere

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:34.827
at a normal temperature.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:35.910
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.302
It's part of the sun's--

00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:38.760
it's part of the
earth's atmosphere

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.170
that keeps our temperature
warm and normal.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:41.670
OK.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.420
Anybody else?

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.780
What is the greenhouse effect?

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.280
Yes?

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.070
Go ahead.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.710
AUDIENCE: So there
are certain gases

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.940
that trap heat and
release that heat

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.690
after it's trapped--
it's something called

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.900
the radiative forcing.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.300
And so some-- for a lot of
gases in the atmosphere,

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.900
like nitrogen, sun rays
just pass right through.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.940
But for a select few,
like water and carbon

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.300
dioxide, that absorb
infrared light and then

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.018
for red spectrum, and then that
traps heat in the atmosphere

00:02:10.018 --> 00:02:11.310
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Fantastic.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.030
You must know your
climate science.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.210
But that's exactly right.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.950
And the basic idea
is that some gases

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.117
in our atmosphere trap heat.

00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:21.600
And you can see here how light
from the sun hits the Earth.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.310
It gets converted into heat.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.900
And it gets trapped underneath
this atmospheric blanket.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.020
We emit these greenhouse
gases by burning fossil fuels

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.450
for transportation
and electricity.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.270
Things like driving,
flying, air conditioning.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:40.620
These all emit greenhouse
gases into the atmosphere

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.920
as we burn fossil fuels like
oil, gas, and coal, greenhouse

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.600
gas concentrations rise.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.767
You can see here
that since 2005,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.100
the amount of carbon dioxide,
one of the main greenhouse

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.650
gases, has increased steadily.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.900
Here's that same
graph since 1700.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.457
You can see the Industrial
Revolution happening in 1760,

00:02:59.457 --> 00:03:01.040
and shortly thereafter
we start to see

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.230
this increase in the
amount of carbon dioxide

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.420
in the atmosphere.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.910
Here's the last 800,000 years.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.180
Now notice in the
last 800,000 years,

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.290
the maximum carbon dioxide level
has been 300 parts per million.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.890
300,000 years ago is the
oldest Homo sapiens fossil.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.840
That's us.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.290
The Industrial Revolution
happened in 1760,

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.730
a little under 300 years ago.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.447
And here's where we are now.

00:03:28.447 --> 00:03:30.155
Atmospheric greenhouse
gases have spiked.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.500
As humans emit more
greenhouse gases,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.045
our atmosphere traps more heat
and the earth gets warmer.

00:03:39.045 --> 00:03:40.670
You can see here the
global temperature

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.197
anomaly from NASA over time.

00:03:43.197 --> 00:03:44.780
It starts with lots
of blues and reds,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.250
meaning things
are pretty normal.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.500
But as time goes on
from 1950 to today,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.490
it gets redder and
redder and redder.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:58.370
That's earth warming to 0.82
degrees Celsius above normal,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.080
or 1.5 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.023
Now, I know what
you're thinking.

00:04:03.023 --> 00:04:04.190
I know what you're thinking.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.080
Who cares about 1.5
degrees Fahrenheit?

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.965
That's like nothing and I
can barely even feel it.

00:04:08.965 --> 00:04:10.340
Well, to answer
this question, we

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.740
have to talk about
the consequences.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.950
Climate change
already affects us.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.959
Whether it's through
wildfires in California,

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:21.480
or flooding in the Midwest, or
hurricanes on the east coast,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.180
the effects of climate
change are already here.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.100
Heat waves, floods,
and other disasters

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.420
are becoming more frequent.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.000
You can see the recorded
natural disasters per year

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.110
has skyrocketed since the 1960s.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.970
In Boston, the city
is already planning

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.310
seawalls because of more
intense and frequent flooding.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.910
We are already
doing these things.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.070
In other words, cities must
spend your parents' tax

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:48.000
money on climate adaptations
instead of schools, transit,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.530
and hospitals.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.450
Unusual weather
also affects food.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.567
By 2030, a drought could
double the price of corn.

00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:58.400
And maybe you don't
like corn, but I bet you

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.130
like chicken or fish, which
are both raised on corn feed.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.580
Or maybe you like high fructose
corn syrup in cereal or bread.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.920
The point is corn is
in a lot of foods,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.260
and by stifling crop
growth climate change

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.910
limits food access.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.500
Of course, it's not
just corn either.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.250
But at any rate, climate
change also affects water.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.420
This woman migrated over 150
miles because of a drought.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:28.443
By 2050, the UN projects 200
million environmental migrants.

00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:30.360
These are people who
leave their homes because

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.960
of changes in the environment.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.326
And my question to you is this.

00:05:34.326 --> 00:05:36.960
In recent migrant crises,
thousands of refugees

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.540
created turmoil in
Europe and America.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.460
What happens when
millions of people

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.450
move north in search of
food, water, and safety?

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.848
Can we handle that?

00:05:47.848 --> 00:05:48.390
I'm not sure.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.230
Even the US military
refers to climate change

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.660
as a threat multiplier,
because it makes

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:56.890
every bad situation worse.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.840
And by the way, the
Pentagon has released

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.660
reports about climate change
under both Democratic and

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:02.920
Republican administrations.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:06.338
This is not a partisan issue.

00:06:06.338 --> 00:06:08.380
These problems are not
just in the future either.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:11.080
They're happening right now.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.850
The current species
extinction rate

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.920
is 1,000 times
higher than normal.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.750
If pollinators go, so do we.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:19.150
There's no more crops left.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.685
We're on track for one
million species extinctions,

00:06:21.685 --> 00:06:23.560
and here's what just
one of those looks like.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:36.716
We could live more
sustainably, so why don't we?

00:06:36.716 --> 00:06:38.540
And to really
understand this, we

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.168
have to talk about the myths.

00:06:41.168 --> 00:06:43.460
And I want to set the stage
with a game called Commons.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:44.877
So at this point,
I want everybody

00:06:44.877 --> 00:06:47.170
to form groups of five and
count off from one to five.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:48.378
Go ahead and sit in a circle.

00:06:48.378 --> 00:06:50.240
You guys can stand
up, form groups.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.070
I'll give you a second
to make this happen.

00:06:56.687 --> 00:06:59.270
It might be easier for people
in the front row to kind of move

00:06:59.270 --> 00:06:59.770
to--

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:02.453
turn your chairs
to face the back.

00:07:02.453 --> 00:07:04.370
Because we're going to
be sitting in a circle.

00:07:08.978 --> 00:07:11.300
Cool.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.180
OK.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.946
And everybody count off from
1 to 5 within your groups.

00:07:14.946 --> 00:07:15.874
AUDIENCE: 1.

00:07:15.874 --> 00:07:16.374
AUDIENCE: 2.

00:07:16.374 --> 00:07:16.850
AUDIENCE: 3.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:17.350
AUDIENCE: 4.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:18.278
AUDIENCE: 1.

00:07:18.278 --> 00:07:19.206
AUDIENCE: 2.

00:07:19.206 --> 00:07:19.706
AUDIENCE: 3.

00:07:19.706 --> 00:07:20.206
AUDIENCE: 4.

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.270
AUDIENCE: 5.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.620
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: OK.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.157
Has everybody counted off?

00:07:26.157 --> 00:07:26.740
We good to go?

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.210
OK.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:32.650
So you're going to all
start a pond with 20 fish.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.770
This is your pond's
carrying capacity.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:38.155
Your goal at the end
of the game, the winner

00:07:38.155 --> 00:07:40.030
is the person in the
room with the most fish.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:44.260
And remember, you win as an
individual, not as a group.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.420
Here's how the game
actually works.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.370
Each round, every
person in your group

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:49.870
will take between
one and four fish.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:51.453
You have to take at
least one, but you

00:07:51.453 --> 00:07:53.620
can't take more than four.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.270
After each round,
your pond will be

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:59.320
restocked by doubling the number
of fish without exceeding 20.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.800
So four fish becomes 8,
nine fish becomes 18,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.080
10 fish or more brings
the pond back to 20.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.880
Does that make sense?

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.750
For the group of
four in the back,

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.180
your carrying
capacity is actually

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.597
going to be four times the
number of people in your group,

00:08:12.597 --> 00:08:14.260
so you're going to have 16.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.870
And so all the same
rules apply where

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:20.560
if you double the number
of fish, at eight or higher

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.360
you'll go back to 16.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.120
You will never have a
pond with 20 fish in it.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.310
Does that make sense
to the group of four?

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.140
Great.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.510
Don't eat the goldfish yet.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.242
And if you have allergies,
don't eat the goldfish at all.

00:08:33.242 --> 00:08:35.200
So if everybody could
send person number one up

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.800
to the front, I can
hand you guys goldfish.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:55.260
Thank you.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:58.070
Great.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:01.850
So everybody should have a
pond and a restock supply.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.480
And can everybody just make sure
that you have the right number

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:05.355
of fish in your pond?

00:09:05.355 --> 00:09:08.298
So it's four times the number
of people in your group.

00:09:08.298 --> 00:09:09.340
Everybody just make sure.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:16.640
Everybody good?

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.800
OK.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.950
You guys have 30 seconds to
discuss strategy starting now.

00:09:24.782 --> 00:09:26.570
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:24.170
Round one.

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.820
Take turns going clockwise,
starting with player three.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:30.130
You have 60 seconds.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.186
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:48.532
Go ahead and restock your ponds.

00:10:48.532 --> 00:10:50.240
You have 30 seconds
to do this each time.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:55.780
You'll play four rounds.

00:11:00.885 --> 00:11:01.510
Everybody good?

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.550
Gonna go ahead and skip.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.770
Round two, starting
with player two.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:11.535
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:11:33.714 --> 00:11:35.950
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:36.520
Restock.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:37.720
You have 30 seconds.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.080
AUDIENCE: Adding 11 this time.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.318
AUDIENCE: No, you
only get to double.

00:11:42.318 --> 00:11:43.110
AUDIENCE: Oh, shit.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:44.530
So we're going [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:57.786 --> 00:11:59.415
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:11:59.415 --> 00:12:00.790
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Round three.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.545
Take turns, starting
with player four.

00:12:08.537 --> 00:12:09.870
AUDIENCE: How many did you take?

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:10.453
AUDIENCE: Two.

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.300
AUDIENCE: Oh, what a guy.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:16.430
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.774
Does anybody need more time?

00:12:36.774 --> 00:12:38.316
I'm going to go
ahead and skip again.

00:12:38.316 --> 00:12:40.020
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.647
Go ahead and restock.

00:12:41.647 --> 00:12:44.389
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.290
Everybody good?

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:03.990
Cool.

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:05.950
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:06.940
Final round.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:08.486
Start with player three.

00:13:08.486 --> 00:13:11.462
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:13:22.772 --> 00:13:23.980
AUDIENCE: What are you doing?

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:25.341
AUDIENCE: It's the last turn.

00:13:25.341 --> 00:13:27.596
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.255
AUDIENCE: Did you take two?

00:13:34.255 --> 00:13:35.990
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.960
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Is everybody set?

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:00.050
Anybody need more time?

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:00.640
Cool.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.318
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:14:03.318 --> 00:14:04.860
Let's do one last
round just for fun.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:05.846
AUDIENCE: No!

00:14:05.846 --> 00:14:08.311
[LAUGHING]

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.340
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: So who
still has fish in their pond?

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:17.840
Cool.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:18.100
OK.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:19.400
So we're actually doing this.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:21.350
Sometimes we don't.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:22.730
Go ahead and restock your ponds.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:23.810
You guys have 30 seconds.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:26.270
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.472
Are you guys all set?

00:14:40.472 --> 00:14:41.430
You guys are restocked?

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.360
AUDIENCE: We already started.

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:42.970
Yeah.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:43.530
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.700
All right, I'm going to
go out and skip ahead.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:45.283
AUDIENCE: Yep.

00:14:45.283 --> 00:14:47.053
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:14:47.053 --> 00:14:49.220
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Final
round for real this time.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.410
Take turns going clockwise,
starting with player number

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:51.910
one.

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:53.820
[STUDENTS ALL SPEAKING]

00:14:58.580 --> 00:14:59.620
All right.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.630
Is everybody good?

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:02.492
Cool.

00:15:02.492 --> 00:15:03.950
AUDIENCE: Is it
for real this time?

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:05.242
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: All right.

00:15:05.242 --> 00:15:06.110
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:07.040
So who won?

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.260
Raise your hand if you
have more than 10 fish.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.890
Sorry, go ahead and
count your fish first,

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:15.490
and then raise your hand
if you have more than 10.

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.090
Cool.

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:18.280
Yeah.

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:19.400
OK.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.830
Only two people have more
than 10, three people, OK.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.510
More than 11 fish?

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:25.340
OK.

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.025
So you guys each
have 12 or more.

00:15:27.025 --> 00:15:29.210
Do you have 13 or more fish?

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.220
14 or more fish?

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:32.600
15 or more fish?

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:34.900
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.650
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
How many do you have?

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:37.450
AUDIENCE: 15.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:38.450
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: 15.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:39.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.920
Congratulations to the winner.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:44.990
Great.

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:46.790
OK.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:47.930
Awesome.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:49.840
OK.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:50.540
So talk to me.

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:51.620
Why was Commons hard?

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.775
What was hard about this game?

00:15:53.775 --> 00:15:56.150
You guys can go ahead and yell
it out or raise your hands

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:56.978
as you wish.

00:15:56.978 --> 00:15:58.520
AUDIENCE: You have
to have a strategy

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:00.380
that everyone agrees upon.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:01.130
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: You
have to have a strategy

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.213
that everyone agrees upon.

00:16:02.213 --> 00:16:02.900
OK.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.130
Anybody else?

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:06.670
What was hard about this game?

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:07.170
Go ahead.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:09.378
AUDIENCE: The thing that
was the best for the commons

00:16:09.378 --> 00:16:10.880
was the most
damaging personally.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:11.620
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
The thing that

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:14.170
was best for the commons was
the most damaging personally.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.770
Anybody else?

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:16.270
Yes.

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:19.897
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
it was unstable.

00:16:19.897 --> 00:16:20.980
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.150
So you weren't
quite sure how long

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:22.830
we were going to keep going.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:23.330
Great.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:24.580
These are all good answers.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:28.060
And really the core of
this is if everyone acts

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:32.720
in their own interest, the
pond, the commons, dies.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.510
This is called the
tragedy of the commons.

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:38.630
When anyone can access a
limited resource, it runs out.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:40.400
Roads are another
really good example.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.090
Anybody with a car
can access a road.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:47.723
And soon enough
everybody is thinking,

00:16:47.723 --> 00:16:49.390
if these idiots would
just take the bus,

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.180
I could be home by now.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:54.670
Can somebody or can anybody
else name some other real life

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:56.140
examples of commons?

00:16:56.140 --> 00:17:00.410
Think about limited resources
that anyone can access.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:01.150
Yes, go ahead.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:01.970
AUDIENCE: Air.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:03.012
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Air.

00:17:03.012 --> 00:17:04.099
Say more.

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:05.420
Can you say more about--

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:08.420
AUDIENCE: Air pollution, how
we're-- just like you were

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:11.390
talking about earlier, how we're
abusing it and overusing it

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:14.218
with all these resources that
we think we need or want.

00:17:14.218 --> 00:17:15.260
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Yes.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.468
So anybody can access the
air, and therefore, anybody

00:17:17.468 --> 00:17:18.170
can pollute it.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:18.680
Great.

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.579
Anybody else?

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:21.746
AUDIENCE: Wireless internet.

00:17:21.746 --> 00:17:23.537
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
OK, wireless internet.

00:17:23.537 --> 00:17:24.589
Say more.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:27.859
AUDIENCE: So if everybody
watches Netflix in high HD

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:30.800
at the same time, nobody
can watch Netflix in HD.

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.300
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
So if everybody

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:33.980
watches Netflix
in HD, then nobody

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:35.180
gets to watch Netflix in HD.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:36.110
Because you don't
have enough bandwidth.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:36.810
Great.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:37.310
Any others?

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:38.440
Let's do one last one.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.470
Any takers?

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.200
Yes, go ahead.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:42.500
AUDIENCE: Office mugs.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:43.430
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Office mugs.

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:43.940
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:45.535
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Tell
me more about office mugs.

00:17:45.535 --> 00:17:47.820
AUDIENCE: If you have
reusable mugs in your office,

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.780
you have to rely on people
cleaning them and putting them

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:51.580
back for everyone else
to be able to use them.

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:52.160
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Great.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:54.452
So office mugs are something
that anybody in the office

00:17:54.452 --> 00:17:56.000
can access, but
nobody's incentivized

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.250
to actually take care of them.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.100
Great.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:00.620
What about this one?

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:01.670
Earth is a commons.

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.200
We can all access earth.

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:05.123
We're all on it.

00:18:05.123 --> 00:18:06.290
And certainly, it's limited.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:11.473
So how do we solve commons
problems, either in the game

00:18:11.473 --> 00:18:12.140
or in real life?

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:15.067
Does anybody have any ideas?

00:18:15.067 --> 00:18:16.650
How did you guys
solve it in the game?

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.500
Let's start there.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:19.820
AUDIENCE: Strategize together.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:20.820
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Strategize together.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:21.440
Cool.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:22.950
Can you be more specific?

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.680
AUDIENCE: We talked
about how we would

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:29.480
be able to keep the most fish
on our plate or in our pond

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:30.480
by each just taking two.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.188
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
So you guys you guys

00:18:32.188 --> 00:18:33.420
all ended up each taking two.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:34.670
AUDIENCE: Not the entire time.

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:35.630
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Not the entire time.

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:36.378
OK.

00:18:36.378 --> 00:18:36.920
Anybody else?

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:39.813
Why-- how do we solve common
problems, either in the game

00:18:39.813 --> 00:18:40.480
or in real life?

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.438
What do you guys think?

00:18:45.787 --> 00:18:46.370
Yes, go ahead.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:49.083
AUDIENCE: If some people took
more, others would take less.

00:18:49.083 --> 00:18:50.750
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
So if some people--

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:52.792
if some people took more,
others would take less.

00:18:52.792 --> 00:18:53.300
Great.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:54.050
How did that work?

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:55.240
AUDIENCE: Not well.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:56.490
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Not well.

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:57.260
OK.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.630
So it didn't work very well.

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:02.930
Well, the answer is, and when
we kind of came upon it already,

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.730
collective action.

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:08.090
We have to set rules in order
to solve common problems.

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:10.260
One example was,
let's all take two.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:13.130
But we saw in your case
that sometimes those rules

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:15.290
get broken.

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:18.568
That's why climate
change is really hard.

00:19:18.568 --> 00:19:20.360
But at any rate, setting
rules for everyone

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:22.460
is called politics.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.950
Setting rules for everyone
is called politics,

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:27.800
and, therefore, protecting
the most important commons,

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:30.440
an inhabitable
earth, is political.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:33.340
We need rules.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.750
There is an issue.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:37.560
Rules cost money.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:39.830
For example, if I
can't sell my products,

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:43.740
because I emit carbon to
make them, I lose money,

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.380
and that's not acceptable to me.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:47.300
So now we can
answer the question,

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:48.890
why haven't we done anything?

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:53.690
And the answer is, no one
wants rules limiting carbon.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.520
But especially not
fossil fuel groups

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.410
who emit more than anyone.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:00.260
You can see here, carbon
dioxide emissions by sector.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:03.270
Residential and commercial
industry, transportation,

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:04.910
agriculture and land use.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:09.040
Anybody want to guess
what that big blue one is?

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:10.010
AUDIENCE: Fossil fuels.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.427
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
Fossil fuels.

00:20:11.427 --> 00:20:15.100
Energy, oil, gas, and coal.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.038
Yeah.

00:20:17.038 --> 00:20:18.580
The fossil fuel
industry has actually

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:21.370
known about climate
change for decades.

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:25.960
In 1978, more than 40 years
ago, Exxon's senior scientists

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.550
had this to say:

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.500
"Present thinking
holds that man has

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:33.100
a time window of 5 to
10 years before the need

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:36.700
for hard decisions regarding
changes in energy strategies

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.140
might become critical."

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:39.860
In other words, we
have five or 10 years

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:41.860
to figure out how to get
energy a different way.

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:43.390
He said that 40 years ago.

00:20:46.072 --> 00:20:50.090
But fossil fuel companies spread
climate myths to the public,

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:50.840
including schools.

00:20:57.520 --> 00:21:00.520
From 2003 to 2010,
Koch Family Foundations

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:05.650
and other fossil fuel groups
gave 558 million climate denial

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:07.440
organizations.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.148
And I wish that I could share
more financial data with you,

00:21:10.148 --> 00:21:12.190
but these groups are
secretive and hard to study.

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.250
In any case, the point is this.

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:20.790
Fossil fuel companies know that
rules will hurt their profits,

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:24.670
so they spread myths to the
public about climate change.

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:26.310
But now you know the truth.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:28.650
You know that climate
change is real.

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:29.880
It's happening because of us.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.230
It already affects you.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.210
It will only get worse.

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:36.060
And we haven't done anything,
because fossil fuel companies

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:37.670
have lied to us for decades.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:42.410
That leaves us with
one final question.

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:43.850
How do we shape our future?

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.950
We need to talk.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.510
And I know that words
may seem inconsequential,

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.520
given some of the other
information I've shared.

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:04.010
But words give life to ideas
and ideas lead to change.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.580
In other words,
your words matter.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.440
I know it may feel
awkward, but studies

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:13.840
show that teens are actually
great at making parents

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.160
care about climate change.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:15.880
Be open.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:16.630
Be gentle.

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:17.440
Be honest.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.300
You can try starting
with, "How do you

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:20.660
feel about climate change?"

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:23.602
In fact, on the count of three,
I want everybody to practice.

00:22:23.602 --> 00:22:25.060
So on the count of
three, let's all

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:26.980
say, "How do you feel
about climate change?"

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:27.480
Ready?

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:28.840
One, two, three,

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:32.410
AUDIENCE: How do you feel
about climate change?

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:36.500
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
It's easy and it works.

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:38.840
I want to share one
last perspective

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:39.890
on why we need to talk.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:50.198
It's not too late to
keep our paradise,

00:22:50.198 --> 00:22:51.490
but we can't do it without you.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.690
I'm going to leave you guys
with a five day challenge.

00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:58.273
So go ahead and
write down the names

00:22:58.273 --> 00:23:00.440
of five people you can talk
to about climate change.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:01.660
You can do it on your phones.

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.130
You can do it on
a piece of paper.

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:04.750
But go ahead and
write down five names

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:06.708
of people you can talk
to about climate change.

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:15.830
Each day, each day for
the next five days,

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:19.190
talk about climate change
with someone on your list.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:24.200
And remember, time
is of the essence.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.934
Thank you.

00:23:25.934 --> 00:23:28.324
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:34.068
Questions?

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.900
AUDIENCE: I noticed the
video presentation included

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.680
afforestation and reforestation
for the large solutions

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:44.620
to this problem.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:46.638
Are there others
that we can consider?

00:23:46.638 --> 00:23:48.430
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
A lot of the solutions

00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:50.440
are collective based.

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:54.640
And by the way, I'll be
sending out a handout

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:56.710
to all of you guys
for things you can do

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:58.780
and informational
resources that you

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:01.000
can use for after this talk.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.790
But to answer your
question, the main thing

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:08.500
that you can do in your life
is actually stop eating beef.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:12.340
Cattle have a huge impact
on greenhouse gases because

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:14.500
of the methane they produce.

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.230
And so if you stop eating
beef, that's actually

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.000
the single biggest
contribution you

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.100
can make within
your personal life.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:25.390
Obviously, if you are able
to drive less, fly less,

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:27.640
that's another huge one.

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:29.830
These are all ways that
you can contribute.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:32.290
But as we saw, part of the
reason that climate change

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:35.050
is such a tricky problem
because we need everybody

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:36.190
to come together.

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:37.190
We need wind farms.

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:39.910
We need afforestation,
as you mentioned.

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:44.830
We need other kinds of
renewable energy resources.

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.560
But in your personal
life, don't eat beef,

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:52.110
talk about climate change, and
fly less are the top three.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:53.130
Yes, go ahead.

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.010
AUDIENCE: We talk a lot about
fossil fuels and transportation

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.980
and flying and things like
that, and same with beef.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.270
We hear a lot about that.

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:03.290
And now people are starting
to hear more about plastics,

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:04.968
which is a use of fossil fuels.

00:25:04.968 --> 00:25:06.510
But there's a lot
of fossil fuel uses

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.900
that are huge, like in
manufacturing processes

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:12.120
and things where we
could buy from companies

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.020
who use less fossil fuels.

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:17.130
But it's really hard for us to
find information about that.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.333
Is there some sort
of resource for that?

00:25:19.333 --> 00:25:21.000
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
I haven't seen any,

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.190
but I can follow up with you.

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:26.900
So I can send you
something after--

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:29.670
along with the handouts,
I'll have a answer for that.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:31.470
I'll do the best that I can.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:33.880
Thanks.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:35.250
Yeah?

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.145
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
parents would

00:25:37.145 --> 00:25:41.610
you recommend a strategy
where you use the spiritual

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:45.400
[INAUDIBLE] argument that God's
given us this world to protect,

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.520
and it's a paradise and
we must protect nature.

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:50.510
Or the self-interest argument.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:52.770
How do you think we should
lead that conversation?

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:53.560
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY:
That's a great question.

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:56.070
So the question is, how
should we talk to our parents

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:57.900
about climate change.

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:59.910
You know your parents
better than I do,

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:03.840
and that's part of why it turns
out that teenagers and kids are

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:05.250
actually so good at this.

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:07.497
Messenger matters
much more than message

00:26:07.497 --> 00:26:08.830
when it comes to climate change.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.870
And so if you come
to your parent

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:12.940
and say this is
important to you,

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:15.690
I think that's enough
to start a conversation.

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.720
Don't attack people,
including your parents.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:22.320
That tends not to
be super helpful.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:24.210
To quote one of the
professors here at MIT,

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:27.000
research shows that
showing research to people

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.690
doesn't work.

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:31.140
And so if you can
talk to your parents

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.000
from a personal
perspective and what

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.820
it means to you, that,
I think, is the best way

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:37.620
to start these conversations.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:40.770
Stories and personal
impacts work a lot better

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:43.760
than graphs and data.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:44.550
Yeah.

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:45.260
Does that answer your question?

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:45.646
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:26:45.646 --> 00:26:46.729
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:26:46.729 --> 00:26:47.988
Thanks.

00:26:47.988 --> 00:26:48.530
Anybody else?

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:51.910
Awesome.

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:52.930
Oh, yes.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:53.740
One more.

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:56.230
AUDIENCE: So what if you
come up against someone

00:26:56.230 --> 00:27:00.810
who insists that it's not real?

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:03.420
How should I talk to
somebody who says,

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.235
well, I've been brainwashed.

00:27:06.235 --> 00:27:08.360
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: That's
a really hard question.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.027
The question is how
do we talk to people

00:27:10.027 --> 00:27:13.940
who are truly convinced that
climate change is not real.

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:16.310
If it's a stranger,
honestly, you're

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:18.890
probably not going to have much
of a shot at convincing them.

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:21.050
As I said, messenger
matters more than message,

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:22.467
and there's not--
you don't really

00:27:22.467 --> 00:27:24.107
have the credibility
with that person

00:27:24.107 --> 00:27:25.940
to sway their opinion
on something that they

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:27.350
hold so dearly.

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:29.720
But, again, if it's a relative,
start the conversation

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:31.610
with what climate
change means for you.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:33.890
And as I said, I'll
send out resources

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:38.630
from NASA and from NOA and from
a bunch of other very credible

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:44.320
institutions that you can look
at to see more information

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:45.655
and why our models work.

00:27:45.655 --> 00:27:47.030
And how we know
all of the things

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:48.680
that we know about
climate change.

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:50.540
And, of course, you're
more than welcome,

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:53.600
in fact, encouraged to share
that with your relatives

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:54.960
and friends and so on.

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:56.252
Does that answer your question?

00:27:56.252 --> 00:27:56.900
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.192
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Thank you.

00:27:58.192 --> 00:27:59.090
Do we have-- no.

00:27:59.090 --> 00:27:59.909
Yes, in the back.

00:27:59.909 --> 00:28:02.034
AUDIENCE: So it was a really
well done presentation

00:28:02.034 --> 00:28:03.770
and I appreciated it.

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:07.360
My question is really
more form than anything.

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:10.430
Whenever you ask the question
about greenhouse gas effect,

00:28:10.430 --> 00:28:13.148
is there like a number in your
head that you have before--

00:28:13.148 --> 00:28:14.690
you'll ask a certain
number of people

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:16.690
before you just move on
for the sake of time?

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:17.773
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:28:17.773 --> 00:28:20.600
I want to hold logistical
questions for afterwards,

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:22.640
but does anybody
have any questions

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:25.940
within the scope of
the presentation?

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:27.362
Anybody else?

00:28:27.362 --> 00:28:28.777
OK.

00:28:28.777 --> 00:28:30.360
AUDIENCE: Honestly
my, like, two cents

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.015
on Frank's question,
which is coming

00:28:33.015 --> 00:28:34.890
from a very conservative
area of the country.

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:37.890
And a very conservative family
that not being confrontational

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:40.540
and just saying, this is how
I feel about climate change

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:42.960
and what's going on has actually
proven to have an effect.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:44.760
Over years, yes.

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:45.930
It's been a slow process.

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:47.922
But saying the data
you're getting--

00:28:47.922 --> 00:28:50.130
where you're getting this
information from is biased.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:52.650
If you look elsewhere,
you'll see different data.

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:53.890
And then I just leave it.

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:56.440
And over time, they started
to see that and have started

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:58.010
to change their minds about it.

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:00.580
So that would be my two cents
from personal experience.

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:01.080
BRANDON LESCHINSKIY: Yeah.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:02.205
That's a really good point.

00:29:02.205 --> 00:29:05.310
So don't expect for
a single conversation

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:09.630
to completely change
somebody's mind.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:14.160
That's really hard and unlikely.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:17.520
But over the course of
these conversations,

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:21.690
I think you'll be surprised
and pleased at how successful

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:23.700
you'll be at changing
the minds of people

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:24.617
that are close to you.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.290
Cool.

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:29.770
Thank you guys so
much for coming.

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:31.720
I really appreciate it.

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:33.720
And we can answer some
logistical questions,

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.095
but we don't need
to record that.

00:29:37.387 --> 00:29:38.220
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:40.970
[APPLAUSE]

